<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#druk-desi">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Druk Desi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1650s–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Druk Desi was the civil ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> under the dual system of government created by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a>, the Zhabdrung, in the seventeenth century, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a> headed the monastic body. In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries the office was often fought over by the regional governors (penlops). It was abolished in 1907, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugyen-wangchuck">Ugyen Wangchuck</a> became hereditary king.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title, in Wylie transliteration 'brug sde-srid and also known as Deb Raja, joins Druk, the thunder dragon that stands for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a>, whose oldest name is Druk-yul, with Desi, meaning regent. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a>, the Zhabdrung Rinpoche, had fled sectarian persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and made the Drukpa Lineage the state religion. Under his dual system the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/je-khenpo">Je Khenpo</a> led the religious branch and the Druk Desi the civil administration, both under the nominal authority of the Zhabdrung, held to be a reincarnation of Ngawang Namgyal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The Druk Desi could be a monk or a layman, and by the nineteenth century was usually a layman. He was elected for a three-year term, at first by a monastic council and later by the State Council, the Lhengye Tshokdu, which brought together regional rulers, the Zhabdrung's chamberlains and the Druk Desi himself. In time the office fell under the control of the council's strongest faction of regional administrators.</p><p>Government sat at the dzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thimphu">Thimphu</a> in spring, summer and autumn and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punakha">Punakha</a>, a dzong founded in 1527, in winter. The kingdom was divided into eastern, central and western regions, each under a penlop who acted as tax collector, judge, military commander and procurer for the centre, drawing revenue from land taxes and the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Districts were run by dzongpens from lesser dzongs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngawang-namgyal">Ngawang Namgyal</a> in 1651 is believed to have been hidden for some 50 years while a reincarnation was sought. In that time the Druk Desi gained power, and civil wars followed; once a successor was found, the Desi would not give up what he had won, and the Zhabdrung was often a child in his control. The Desi in turn lost his hold over the penlops, and the country broke into semi-independent regions. The Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhutan">Bhutan</a> of 2008 keeps the dual system but never names the Druk Desi, vesting administrative power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druk-gyalpo">Druk Gyalpo</a> and civilian offices.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Druk Desi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zhabdrung created the dual system, chhoesi, in which a chief abbot, the Je Khenpo, led the monastic body while a civil ruler, the <b>Druk Desi</b>, governed, with regional governors called penlops.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhabdrung-bhutan">Zhabdrung Bhutan</a></li></ul></section>
